<section xml:id="sec-b-6-practice-problems">
  <title>Practice problems</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-b-6-practice-problems-lines">
    <title>Lines</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>Find the slope of the line through <m>(2,3)</m> and <m>(8,6)</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find the slope of the line through <m>(-1,4)</m> and <m>(5,-8)</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find an equation of the line through <m>(3,-2)</m> with slope <m>4</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find an equation of the line through <m>(1,5)</m> and <m>(4,-1)</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find the slope and <m>y</m>-intercept of</p>
          <md>2x+3y=12.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find an equation of the line through <m>(2,1)</m> parallel to</p>
          <md>y=-3x+4.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find an equation of the line through <m>(-2,5)</m> perpendicular to</p>
          <md>y=\frac12x-1.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find the <m>x</m>- and <m>y</m>-intercepts of</p>
          <md>4x-5y=20.</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-b-6-practice-problems-circles">
    <title>Circles</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>Find an equation of the circle with center <m>(-1,4)</m> and radius <m>6</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find the center and radius of</p>
          <md>(x-3)^2+(y+2)^2=49.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Put the equation</p>
          <md>x^2+y^2+8x-10y+16=0</md>
        <p>in standard circle form.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Describe the region</p>
          <md>(x+1)^2+(y-2)^2&lt;16.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find the intersection points of</p>
          <md>x^2+y^2=25</md>
        <p>and</p>
          <md>y=3.</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-b-6-practice-problems-parabolas-ellipses-and-hyperbolas">
    <title>Parabolas, ellipses, and hyperbolas</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>Rewrite</p>
          <md>y=x^2+4x-1</md>
        <p>in vertex form.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find the vertex and opening direction of</p>
          <md>y=-3(x-2)^2+5.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find the focus and directrix of</p>
          <md>x^2=12y.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Describe the ellipse</p>
          <md>\frac{(x-1)^2}{25}+\frac{(y+3)^2}{4}=1.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Describe the hyperbola</p>
          <md>\frac{(x+2)^2}{16}-\frac{(y-1)^2}{9}=1.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Put</p>
          <md>4x^2+9y^2-16x+18y-11=0</md>
        <p>in standard form and identify the conic.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-b-6-practice-problems-distance-and-midpoint">
    <title>Distance and midpoint</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>Find the distance between <m>(1,-2)</m> and <m>(7,6)</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find the midpoint of the segment joining <m>(-4,9)</m> and <m>(2,-1)</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find the center and radius of the circle whose diameter has endpoints</p>
          <md>(-3,2)
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
(5,8).</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find the set of points equidistant from</p>
          <md>(0,0)
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
(6,0).</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-b-6-practice-problems-scaling-and-shifting-graphs">
    <title>Scaling and shifting graphs</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>Describe how the graph of</p>
          <md>y=(x+4)^2-7</md>
        <p>is obtained from <m>y=x^2</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Describe how the graph of</p>
          <md>y=-2\sqrt{x-5}+3</md>
        <p>is obtained from <m>y=\sqrt{x}</m>.  State its domain and range.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Describe how the graph of</p>
          <md>y=\cos(3x)</md>
        <p>is obtained from <m>y=\cos x</m>.  What is its period?</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find the center and semi-axis lengths of</p>
          <md>\frac{(x-2)^2}{9}+\frac{(y+1)^2}{16}=1.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> contains the point <m>(2,5)</m>.  What point must lie on
the graph of</p>
          <md>y=f(x-3)+4?</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="ans-appendix-b-practice">
    <title>Answers to Appendix B practice problems</title>


  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-b-6-practice-problems-lines-2">
    <title>Lines</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
          <md>m=\frac{6-3}{8-2}=\frac36=\frac12.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>m=\frac{-8-4}{5-(-1)}=\frac{-12}{6}=-2.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>y+2=4(x-3).</md>
        <p>Equivalently,</p>
          <md>y=4x-14.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>m=\frac{-1-5}{4-1}=-2.</md>
        <p>Thus</p>
          <md>y-5=-2(x-1),</md>
        <p>or</p>
          <md>y=-2x+7.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>2x+3y=12</md>
        <p>gives</p>
          <md>3y=-2x+12,</md>
        <p>so</p>
          <md>y=-\frac23x+4.</md>
        <p>The slope is <m>-2/3</m>, and the <m>y</m>-intercept is <m>4</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>A parallel line has slope <m>-3</m>.  Through <m>(2,1)</m>,</p>
          <md>y-1=-3(x-2).</md>
        <p>So</p>
          <md>y=-3x+7.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The given slope is <m>1/2</m>, so the perpendicular slope is <m>-2</m>.  Through
<m>(-2,5)</m>,</p>
          <md>y-5=-2(x+2).</md>
        <p>So</p>
          <md>y=-2x+1.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>For the <m>x</m>-intercept, set <m>y=0</m>:</p>
          <md>4x=20,</md>
        <p>so</p>
          <md>x=5.</md>
        <p>Thus the <m>x</m>-intercept is <m>(5,0)</m>.</p>
        <p>For the <m>y</m>-intercept, set <m>x=0</m>:</p>
          <md>-5y=20,</md>
        <p>so</p>
          <md>y=-4.</md>
        <p>Thus the <m>y</m>-intercept is <m>(0,-4)</m>.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-b-6-practice-problems-circles-2">
    <title>Circles</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
          <md>(x+1)^2+(y-4)^2=36.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The center is</p>
          <md>(3,-2),</md>
        <p>and the radius is</p>
          <md>7.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Start with</p>
          <md>x^2+y^2+8x-10y+16=0.</md>
        <p>Move the constant:</p>
          <md>x^2+8x+y^2-10y=-16.</md>
        <p>Complete squares:</p>
          <md>x^2+8x=(x+4)^2-16,</md>
        <p>and</p>
          <md>y^2-10y=(y-5)^2-25.</md>
        <p>So</p>
          <md>(x+4)^2-16+(y-5)^2-25=-16.</md>
        <p>Thus</p>
          <md>(x+4)^2+(y-5)^2=25.</md>
        <p>The center is <m>(-4,5)</m>, and the radius is <m>5</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>This is the open disk centered at</p>
          <md>(-1,2)</md>
        <p>with radius <m>4</m>.  The boundary circle is not included.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Substitute <m>y=3</m> into <m>x^2+y^2=25</m>:</p>
          <md>x^2+9=25.</md>
        <p>So</p>
          <md>x^2=16,</md>
        <p>and</p>
          <md>x=\pm4.</md>
        <p>The intersection points are</p>
          <md>(-4,3)\quad\text{and}\quad(4,3).</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-b-6-practice-problems-parabolas-ellipses-and-hyperbolas-2">
    <title>Parabolas, ellipses, and hyperbolas</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
          <md>y=x^2+4x-1=(x+2)^2-4-1.</md>
        <p>So</p>
          <md>
y=(x+2)^2-5.
</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The vertex is</p>
          <md>(2,5).</md>
        <p>Since the coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Compare</p>
          <md>x^2=12y</md>
        <p>with</p>
          <md>x^2=4py.</md>
        <p>Then</p>
          <md>4p=12,</md>
        <p>so</p>
          <md>p=3.</md>
        <p>The focus is</p>
          <md>(0,3),</md>
        <p>and the directrix is</p>
          <md>y=-3.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The center is</p>
          <md>(1,-3).</md>
        <p>The horizontal semi-axis length is <m>5</m>, and the vertical semi-axis length is <m>2</m>.
The ellipse has width <m>10</m> and height <m>4</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The center is</p>
          <md>(-2,1).</md>
        <p>It opens left and right.  Since</p>
          <md>a=4,\qquad b=3,</md>
        <p>the vertices are</p>
          <md>(-2-4,1)=(-6,1)</md>
        <p>and</p>
          <md>(-2+4,1)=(2,1).</md>
        <p>The asymptotes are</p>
          <md>y-1=\pm\frac34(x+2).</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Start with</p>
          <md>4x^2+9y^2-16x+18y-11=0.</md>
        <p>Group terms:</p>
          <md>4(x^2-4x)+9(y^2+2y)=11.</md>
        <p>Complete squares:</p>
          <md>x^2-4x=(x-2)^2-4,</md>
        <p>and</p>
          <md>y^2+2y=(y+1)^2-1.</md>
        <p>So</p>
          <md>4\bigl((x-2)^2-4\bigr)+9\bigl((y+1)^2-1\bigr)=11.</md>
        <p>Thus</p>
          <md>4(x-2)^2-16+9(y+1)^2-9=11.</md>
        <p>So</p>
          <md>4(x-2)^2+9(y+1)^2=36.</md>
        <p>Divide by <m>36</m>:</p>
          <md>
\frac{(x-2)^2}{9}+\frac{(y+1)^2}{4}=1.
</md>
        <p>This is an ellipse centered at <m>(2,-1)</m>.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-b-6-practice-problems-distance-and-midpoint-2">
    <title>Distance and midpoint</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
          <md>d=\sqrt{(7-1)^2+(6-(-2))^2}
=
\sqrt{6^2+8^2}
=
10.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>M=
\left(
\frac{-4+2}{2},
\frac{9+(-1)}{2}
\right)
=
(-1,4).</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The center is the midpoint:</p>
          <md>M=
\left(
\frac{-3+5}{2},
\frac{2+8}{2}
\right)
=
(1,5).</md>
        <p>The diameter length is</p>
          <md>\sqrt{(5-(-3))^2+(8-2)^2}
=
\sqrt{8^2+6^2}
=
10.</md>
        <p>So the radius is</p>
          <md>5.</md>
        <p>The circle is</p>
          <md>
(x-1)^2+(y-5)^2=25.
</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Let <m>(x,y)</m> be equidistant from <m>(0,0)</m> and <m>(6,0)</m>.  Then</p>
          <md>x^2+y^2=(x-6)^2+y^2.</md>
        <p>Cancel <m>y^2</m>:</p>
          <md>x^2=x^2-12x+36.</md>
        <p>So</p>
          <md>12x=36,</md>
        <p>and</p>
          <md>x=3.</md>
        <p>The locus is the vertical line <m>x=3</m>.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-b-6-practice-problems-scaling-and-shifting-graphs-2">
    <title>Scaling and shifting graphs</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>The graph of <m>y=x^2</m> is shifted left <m>4</m> and down <m>7</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The graph of <m>y=\sqrt{x}</m> is shifted right <m>5</m>, reflected across the <m>x</m>-axis,
stretched vertically by <m>2</m>, and shifted up <m>3</m>.</p>
        <p>The domain is</p>
          <md>[5,\infty).</md>
        <p>Since <m>\sqrt{x-5}\ge0</m>, we have</p>
          <md>-2\sqrt{x-5}+3\le3.</md>
        <p>The range is</p>
          <md>(-\infty,3].</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The graph of <m>y=\cos x</m> is horizontally compressed by <m>3</m>.  Its period is</p>
          <md>\frac{2\pi}{3}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The center is</p>
          <md>(2,-1).</md>
        <p>The horizontal semi-axis length is <m>3</m>, and the vertical semi-axis length is <m>4</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The point <m>(2,5)</m> on <m>y=f(x)</m> means</p>
          <md>f(2)=5.</md>
        <p>For</p>
          <md>y=f(x-3)+4,</md>
        <p>we need</p>
          <md>x-3=2,</md>
        <p>so</p>
          <md>x=5.</md>
        <p>Then</p>
          <md>y=f(2)+4=5+4=9.</md>
        <p>So the new graph contains</p>
          <md>(5,9).</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

</section>
